WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.460
If you've spent years writing code,

2
00:00:02.460 --> 00:00:05.560
you know the sinking feeling of picking up a new framework.

3
00:00:05.560 --> 00:00:08.320
You crack open a basic tutorial and suddenly,

4
00:00:08.320 --> 00:00:11.560
you are being treated like you've never touched a keyboard before.

5
00:00:11.560 --> 00:00:13.680
You already know how to build software.

6
00:00:13.680 --> 00:00:17.360
You might be deep into Kotlin and Jetpack Compose for Android.

7
00:00:17.360 --> 00:00:20.880
Maybe you spend your days writing Swift and Swift UI for iOS,

8
00:00:20.880 --> 00:00:24.040
or managing complex React component trees for the web.

9
00:00:24.040 --> 00:00:26.120
This graph illustrates exactly what

10
00:00:26.120 --> 00:00:28.120
that standard learning path looks like.

11
00:00:28.400 --> 00:00:32.400
Your hard-earned expertise plummets to a generic beginner baseline,

12
00:00:32.400 --> 00:00:35.120
forcing you into a slow, shallow climb through concepts

13
00:00:35.120 --> 00:00:37.200
you actually already understand.

14
00:00:37.200 --> 00:00:40.760
Meanwhile, the industry is not waiting around in 2026.

15
00:00:40.760 --> 00:00:42.600
The demand for developers who can ship

16
00:00:42.600 --> 00:00:45.760
robust cross-platform applications is high,

17
00:00:45.760 --> 00:00:48.000
and engineering teams need to move fast.

18
00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:51.120
That is exactly why we built React Native Academy.

19
00:00:51.120 --> 00:00:53.920
This is a structured system designed exclusively

20
00:00:53.920 --> 00:00:56.240
for developers who already know how to code.

21
00:00:56.320 --> 00:00:59.120
Mastering a new architecture happens much faster

22
00:00:59.120 --> 00:01:01.560
when you use your existing skills as the bridge,

23
00:01:01.560 --> 00:01:03.920
rather than erasing them to start from scratch.

24
00:01:03.920 --> 00:01:06.120
The academy relies on four learning paths

25
00:01:06.120 --> 00:01:07.560
tailored to your background.

26
00:01:07.560 --> 00:01:11.120
The Android Trail maps Jetpack Compose to React Native.

27
00:01:11.120 --> 00:01:14.560
The iOS Trail links Swift to cross-platform paradigms.

28
00:01:14.560 --> 00:01:17.720
The Web Trail applies JSX and hooks to mobile.

29
00:01:17.720 --> 00:01:19.680
The Masterclass Trail skips onboarding

30
00:01:19.680 --> 00:01:21.480
for new architecture fluency.

31
00:01:21.480 --> 00:01:23.400
This entire system runs on a method

32
00:01:23.400 --> 00:01:25.280
called comparative learning.

33
00:01:25.280 --> 00:01:27.760
When we introduce a new React Native concept,

34
00:01:27.760 --> 00:01:30.320
we put it side-by-side with the native or web code

35
00:01:30.320 --> 00:01:32.040
you are already writing today.

36
00:01:32.040 --> 00:01:33.920
By routing you through the exact concepts

37
00:01:33.920 --> 00:01:37.160
you actually need, we cut out the redundant material.

38
00:01:37.160 --> 00:01:38.880
The steep learning curve flattens out

39
00:01:38.880 --> 00:01:41.880
into a direct horizontal translation of your skills.

40
00:01:41.880 --> 00:01:44.280
But even with a tailored curriculum,

41
00:01:44.280 --> 00:01:46.640
mobile development has historically shared

42
00:01:46.640 --> 00:01:49.800
one massive bottleneck, wasting hours

43
00:01:49.800 --> 00:01:53.120
configuring local environments, updating SDKs,

44
00:01:53.120 --> 00:01:57.160
and fighting emulators before writing a single line of code.

45
00:01:57.160 --> 00:02:00.160
Instead of spending your first day installing dependencies,

46
00:02:00.160 --> 00:02:02.200
you move straight to writing components

47
00:02:02.200 --> 00:02:05.360
and compiling them on your screen in seconds.

48
00:02:05.360 --> 00:02:06.840
Every code example in this course

49
00:02:06.840 --> 00:02:10.280
runs directly in your browser using ExpoSnack.

50
00:02:10.280 --> 00:02:12.320
There is absolutely zero local setup

51
00:02:12.320 --> 00:02:14.240
required to begin coding.

52
00:02:14.240 --> 00:02:15.880
Right inside your browser window,

53
00:02:15.880 --> 00:02:18.120
you instantly test front-end layouts,

54
00:02:18.120 --> 00:02:20.280
combining React Native's core components

55
00:02:20.280 --> 00:02:22.200
with strict Flexbox styling

56
00:02:22.200 --> 00:02:24.240
to build actual screens.

57
00:02:24.240 --> 00:02:26.160
You also write strict TypeScript,

58
00:02:26.160 --> 00:02:29.960
handle local state, and test complex screen routing mechanics

59
00:02:29.960 --> 00:02:32.160
using React navigation.

60
00:02:32.160 --> 00:02:35.360
The engine even lets you interact with device hardware.

61
00:02:35.360 --> 00:02:39.280
You call platform-specific APIs through native modules,

62
00:02:39.280 --> 00:02:41.600
accessing the camera, location services,

63
00:02:41.600 --> 00:02:43.480
and local storage without needing

64
00:02:43.480 --> 00:02:46.080
a physical device plugged into your machine.

65
00:02:46.080 --> 00:02:49.720
This creates a tight, consistent loop for every module.

66
00:02:49.720 --> 00:02:51.160
We explain the concept,

67
00:02:51.160 --> 00:02:52.960
we show the platform comparison,

68
00:02:52.960 --> 00:02:56.160
and then you execute the code directly in the browser.

69
00:02:56.160 --> 00:02:57.880
Removing that setup friction means

70
00:02:57.880 --> 00:02:59.560
your entire cognitive budget

71
00:02:59.560 --> 00:03:02.240
goes toward understanding the architecture itself

72
00:03:02.240 --> 00:03:04.760
instead of fighting your operating system.

73
00:03:04.760 --> 00:03:07.280
No matter which of the four trails you start on,

74
00:03:07.280 --> 00:03:10.680
every path converges on the exact same destination.

75
00:03:10.680 --> 00:03:13.840
This blueprint represents the ultimate state of the course,

76
00:03:13.840 --> 00:03:17.600
absolute fluency in React Native's new architecture.

77
00:03:17.600 --> 00:03:19.440
You walk away with a solid foundation

78
00:03:19.440 --> 00:03:22.640
for constructing complex mobile applications.

79
00:03:22.640 --> 00:03:25.240
Practically, this means you will possess the ability

80
00:03:25.240 --> 00:03:28.040
to deploy serious, production-grade applications

81
00:03:28.040 --> 00:03:30.200
to both iOS and Android,

82
00:03:30.200 --> 00:03:33.360
all managed from a single unified code base.

83
00:03:33.360 --> 00:03:35.520
You will read and write TypeScript fluently

84
00:03:35.520 --> 00:03:38.920
in a mobile context, construct pixel-perfect layouts,

85
00:03:38.920 --> 00:03:41.880
and integrate native hardware modules seamlessly.

86
00:03:41.880 --> 00:03:44.320
Understanding these mechanics is crucial

87
00:03:44.320 --> 00:03:46.200
because React Native has matured

88
00:03:46.200 --> 00:03:48.280
into a serious production choice

89
00:03:48.280 --> 00:03:50.880
for enterprise teams in 2026.

90
00:03:50.880 --> 00:03:53.040
The ability to ship to two platforms

91
00:03:53.040 --> 00:03:55.040
without sacrificing native performance

92
00:03:55.040 --> 00:03:57.760
is a massive competitive advantage.

93
00:03:57.760 --> 00:03:59.240
Your immediate next step

94
00:03:59.240 --> 00:04:02.320
is to head to the history and architecture module.

95
00:04:02.320 --> 00:04:05.120
You will learn exactly why the framework evolved this way,

96
00:04:05.120 --> 00:04:08.040
and then you can pick your specific trail.

97
00:04:08.040 --> 00:04:09.640
Your past development experience

98
00:04:09.640 --> 00:04:11.440
is your greatest asset here.

99
00:04:11.440 --> 00:04:13.400
We are going to put it to work.

100
00:04:13.400 --> 00:04:15.360
Welcome to React Native Academy.
